PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B2}
White’s most forcing attack is to take the shared liberty of the black pair on C1–C2.  With Black at A1 A2 C1 C2 B3 B4 the two stones on C1 C2 are already almost settled; their only real breathing room is B1 and B2 and B1 is already covered by the Black wall on A1 and C1.  Playing B2 removes the last open liberty of the C1–C2 pair and at the same time it threatens A1–A2 and B3–B4 by joining the White stones on B5 C3 C4.  

After B2 the C1–C2 pair is in immediate atari and Black has no useful way to increase liberties without playing into White’s net on the left edge.  Any attempt to save the left side, e.g. A3 or A4, leaves the connected Black group with only B1 and A4, and White can then close the net with A3/A4 and finish the capture.  The D1–D2 group is a necessary sacrifice; once the Black net is taken the two stones are already worth less than the five Black stones that are removed.  B2 therefore initiates a forced kill sequence.

